To consume a WCF service in a .NET Core API, you can use the WCF Connected Service extension. Here’s how you can do it:


---

1. Install WCF Connected Service Extension

1. Open your .NET Core API project in Visual Studio.


2. Go to Extensions > Manage Extensions.


3. Search for WCF Connected Service and install it.


4. Restart Visual Studio after installation.




---

2. Add a Reference to the WCF Service

1. Right-click on your project in the Solution Explorer.


2. Select Add > Connected Service.


3. In the dialog box, choose WCF Connected Service.


4. Click Configure and enter the WCF service's WSDL URL (e.g., http://yourservice/service.svc?wsdl).


5. Configure the namespace and other settings as needed.


6. Click Finish to generate the client proxy.




---

3. Install Necessary NuGet Packages

The WCF Connected Service tool adds required packages to your project. Ensure these packages are installed:

System.ServiceModel.Http

System.ServiceModel.Primitives

System.ServiceModel.Security


You can install them manually using the NuGet Package Manager or the Package Manager Console:

dotnet add package System.ServiceModel.Http
dotnet add package System.ServiceModel.Primitives
dotnet add package System.ServiceModel.Security


---

4. Use the Generated Proxy to Call the WCF Service

The WCF Connected Service generates a client class to interact with the WCF service. Use this class in your API controller.

Here’s an example:

Example Code

using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using YourNamespace.ServiceReference; // Replace with your namespace

[Route("api/[controller]")]
[ApiController]
public class WcfClientController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet("GetData")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> GetDataAsync(int value)
    {
        try
        {
            // Create a client instance
            var binding = new BasicHttpBinding();
            var endpoint = new EndpointAddress("http://yourservice/service.svc");
            var client = new ServiceClient(binding, endpoint);

            // Call the WCF method
            var result = await client.GetDataAsync(value);

            // Close the client
            await client.CloseAsync();

            return Ok(result);
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            return StatusCode(500, $"Error: {ex.Message}");
        }
    }
}


---

5. Run and Test the API

1. Start your .NET Core API.


2. Use tools like Postman or cURL to test the endpoint that interacts with the WCF service.




---

6. Error Handling

Handle exceptions such as timeouts and communication errors.

Ensure proper disposal of the WCF client to prevent memory leaks.

Consider adding retry policies using libraries like Polly.


With this setup, your .NET Core API can consume the WCF service seamlessly.

